256 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 137 0 R
/E (This means that in most countries, the hypothesis is not supported: parental education and family cultural capital are more strongly associated with scores than material possessions and money. However, there are countries where wealth matters more than education, such as Lebanon, Cambodia, Zambia, Argentina, Ecuador, and Malaysia. These are mostly lower-income countries, where a family's wealth can determine whether a child has access to a quality school at all.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [3]
